byid={x['id']:x for x in items}
for t in talks:
    if t['record']==435:
        t['date']='1977-09-21'
        t['summary']=r'The catalogue date is retained in the date field, but the manuscript heading is unambiguously 21/XI/1977 (21 November), not 21 September. Manin gives the twistor and finite-dimensional linear-algebra description of unitary instantons, including orthogonal projection, Hermitian Gram matrices and a local connection formula. The matching Drinfeld--Manin announcement was received 27 November 1977 and published in 1978. The question about arbitrary connections already had an affirmative universal-connection theorem.'
x=byid['1977-431.3']
x['class']='E'
x['fate']=r'The underlying construction predates this talk: \citet[Theorem~1 and concluding discussion]{krichever1976zakharov}, received 8 October 1975 and published in 1976, construct the quasiperiodic solutions and announce theta formulas. \citet[\S1, pp.189--191]{krichever1977methods}, received 24 March 1977, give the explicit theta representation and Jacobian parameters. The 1978 restatement \citet[introduction and \S1]{krichever1978commutative} is the closest historical discussion, not the first appearance of the mathematical method.'
x['refs']=['krichever1976zakharov','krichever1977methods','krichever1978commutative']
x=byid['1977-431.2']
x['fate']+=r' The rank-one pointed-curve data also recur in 1975-383.9--383.12 and 1975-385.4--385.8, associated with GP-1975-14. The present historical classification remark does not itself restate the earlier inverse-construction question; GP-1975-26 concerns higher rank and is not an exact recurrence here.'
x=byid['1977-435.6']
x['fate']+=r' Earlier occurrence 1975-382.5 describes the tautological universal connection and its base; it has no earlier problem identifier. That construction and this explicit universality question are closely related, with the quantifiers stated here.'
x=byid['1977-432.8']
x['fate']=r'\citet[p.2662, paragraph preceding the theorem]{atiyahhitchinsinger1977deformations} explicitly attribute the \(5n+4\)-parameter family to Jackiw, Nohl and Rebbi, distinguishing it from the \(5n\)-parameter family of \textquotesingle t Hooft. Their theorem gives \(8n-3\) independent unframed moduli. \citet[\S4]{drinfeldmanin1978selfdual} subsequently give all instantons for the classical compact groups. The notebook conflates the ansatz attribution and raw versus independent parameter counts.'
x['confidence']='high'
x=byid['1977-432.9']
x['fate']=r'\citet[theorem and proof, pp.2662--2663]{atiyahhitchinsinger1977deformations} prove smoothness and dimension \(8k-3\), using the self-dual deformation complex and vanishing of its obstruction space. Contributed 20 April 1977 and published in July 1977, this was available before the talk. Both pages of the primary scanned article were read.'
x['verification']='read the result'
x=byid['1977-432.11']
x['fate']=r'\citet[p.2663, paragraph beginning with the conformal-group action]{atiyahhitchinsinger1977deformations} identify the charge-one moduli space with hyperbolic five-space, the orbit of the basic instanton. The same charge-one case is included in \citet[\S4]{drinfeldmanin1978selfdual}. The notebook\textquotesingle s group-dimension argument alone would not prove uniqueness, but the earlier primary theorem supplies the result.'
x['verification']='read the result';x['publication_match']='exact';x['confidence']='high'
x=byid['1977-434.3']
x['fate']=r'\citet[\S2, Theorem~2 and \S3, Corollary~1]{krichever1978rational} establish the pole/particle correspondence. Their Hamiltonian notation must be compared together with their time and Poisson conventions; no claim of an error in that published theorem is made here. The displayed notebook equation, ansatz and canonical convention \(p=q_y\) give the coefficients in the statement by direct Laurent expansion.'
x=byid['1977-436.11']
x['notes']+=r' Equation (26) writes \([\pi(x),u(y)]=i\delta(x-y)\), in the opposite order to the earlier Poisson bracket.'
x['reconstruction']=r'With the convention used in item 1977-436.1, canonical quantization gives \([u(x),\pi(y)]=i\delta(x-y)\). The source changes the bracket order without specifying a corresponding sign convention.'
x=byid['1977-436.26']
x['fate']=r'\citet{pohlmeyer1976integrable} appeared in 1976, received 28 August 1975. \citet[introduction, \S3 and \S6]{zakharovmikhailov1978relativistic}, submitted 4 January 1978 and published June 1978, explicitly identify Pohlmeyer\textquotesingle s sphere-model result and develop the reduction and integration scheme. Thus the classical claim was already known; the latter article is not treated as a theorem of integrability for all homogeneous spaces.'

